    <h2>How Long Does a Clothing Order Actually Take From Bangladesh?</h2>
    <p>New brands consistently underestimate this. A standard OEM order of 500 to 2,000 pieces from Bangladesh takes around 90 days from the point your tech pack is approved to the moment goods arrive in your warehouse by sea freight. That's not 90 days of the factory being busy — it's 90 days of specific, sequential steps, each of which has to be completed before the next one can start.</p>

    <table class="lt-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Stage</th><th>Who acts</th><th>Typical duration</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Tech pack review and order confirmation</td><td>Buyer and factory</td><td>3–5 days</td></tr>
        <tr><td>30% deposit payment</td><td>Buyer</td><td>1–2 days</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Proto / first sample production</td><td>Factory</td><td>12–18 days</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Buyer review and revision feedback</td><td>Buyer</td><td>5–7 days</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Pre-production sample (in bulk fabric)</td><td>Factory</td><td>7–10 days</td></tr>
        <tr><td>PP sample sign-off</td><td>Buyer</td><td>3–5 days</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Bulk cut and sew production</td><td>Factory</td><td>30–50 days</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Final QC, packing, and export documents</td><td>Factory</td><td>3–5 days</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sea freight (Chittagong to destination port)</td><td>Freight forwarder</td><td>38–45 days</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Customs clearance</td><td>Buyer's broker</td><td>3–7 days</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>ODM and private label orders are meaningfully faster — the factory already has base patterns and fabric relationships in place, so sampling begins sooner. CMT orders skip the fabric sourcing lead time because you supply it yourself. The calculator above adjusts for all of these.</p>

    <h2>The Seasonal Deadlines That Catch Brands Out</h2>
    <p>Working backwards from specific retail seasons, here are rough tech pack submission deadlines for sea freight from Bangladesh. These assume one revision round on the proto sample — add two to three weeks if you anticipate a second round.</p>

    <div class="season-grid">
      <div class="season-item">
        <strong>Christmas / Holiday (US, goods in-hand 1 Dec)</strong>
        <span>Submit tech pack by: early-to-mid July. Container needs to leave Chittagong around 10–15 October.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="season-item">
        <strong>Spring / Summer (EU, goods in-hand 1 Mar)</strong>
        <span>Submit tech pack by: early November of the previous year. Container departure around 20–25 January.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="season-item">
        <strong>Back to School (US, goods in-hand 1 Aug)</strong>
        <span>Submit tech pack by: early-to-mid April. Container departure around 15–20 June.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="season-item">
        <strong>Black Friday (US, goods in-hand 15 Nov)</strong>
        <span>Submit tech pack by: mid-to-late June. Container departure around 25–30 September.</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>The Two Things That Delay Orders the Most</h2>
    <p>In practice, two issues account for the majority of missed deadlines in garment production from Bangladesh, and both are on the buyer's side.</p>
    <p>The first is an incomplete tech pack at the start. If the factory has to ask clarifying questions about measurements, construction details, or trim specifications before they can begin cutting the sample, that back-and-forth can delay the first sample by one to two weeks before a single stitch has been sewn. Brands that submit a clear, complete pack upfront consistently get their orders finished faster.</p>
    <p>The second is slow sample approval. Every additional revision round on the proto sample adds 12 to 18 days to the schedule. Brands that review samples promptly and send precise, written comments — rather than vague feedback over multiple messages — get through the sampling stage without it eating into their bulk production buffer.</p>

    <h2>OEM, ODM, CMT, or Private Label — Which Is Right for Your Timeline?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong style="color:#fff;">OEM</strong> — you provide the full tech pack, the factory builds to it exactly. The most lead time, the most design control. Best if you have in-house design capability and a clear spec.</li>
      <li><strong style="color:#fff;">ODM</strong> — the factory has existing patterns you modify and brand. Sampling is faster because the base exists. Good for brands scaling quickly without a dedicated design team.</li>
      <li><strong style="color:#fff;">CMT</strong> — you source and ship fabric to the factory, which only cuts, sews, and finishes. Saves the fabric sourcing time at the factory end but shifts the sourcing risk to you.</li>
      <li><strong style="color:#fff;">Private Label</strong> — the factory has finished styles you relabel. The fastest route to market, with the least customisation. Good for testing demand before committing to a custom range.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Sea Freight vs Air Freight From Bangladesh</h2>
    <p>Sea freight from Chittagong takes roughly 38 to 45 days to reach most destination ports. Air freight from Dhaka takes around 5 to 7 days. The cost difference is significant — air freight typically runs four to five times more per kilogram than sea, which can add several dollars to your landed cost per unit depending on garment weight.</p>
    <p>One approach that works commercially for some brands is a hybrid: ship the majority of the order by sea for regular replenishment stock, and air freight a smaller launch quantity to hit the opening date while the sea shipment is still in transit. Whether the numbers make sense depends on your margin — the <a href="/tools/garment-cost-calculator" title="Garment cost calculator">Garment Cost Calculator</a> lets you compare the full landed cost under each scenario.</p>

